Abstract

This book reviews the theory and observations of pulsars, with emphasis on a description of phenomena rather than abstruse theory. The discovery of pulsars is recounted, techniques for search and observation are outlined, and the identification of pulsars with rotating neutron stars is discussed. Properties of various X-ray pulsars are described, and the internal structure and magnetosphere of neutron stars are investigated. Pulse timing is examined along with the properties of integrated and individual radio pulses, observations of the Crab Nebula, and characteristics of the Crab Pulsar. Attention is also given to the interstellar medium as an indicator of pulsar distances, the interstellar magnetic field, models and analyses of interstellar scintillation, pulsar radiation processes, and the pulsar emission mechanism. The origin of pulsars in supernova explosions is examined in detail, and the distribution and ages of pulsars are considered. The concept of condensed stars as an energy source is evaluated in relation to X-ray binaries, globular clusters identified with X-ray sources, the Galactic Center, X-ray sources in clusters of galaxies, radio galaxies, and quasars.

